"Today's PHP Security Release, and How We're Strengthening PHP Security — The PHP Foundation — Supporting, Advancing, and Developing the PHP Language (https://thephp.foundation/blog/2026/10/21/todays-php-security-release-and-how-were-strengthening-php-security/)\nciteturn89view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://thephp.foundation/blog/2026/10/21/todays-php-security-release-and-how-were-strengthening-php-security/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 87\nL0: The PHP Foundation\nL1: \nL2: The PHP Foundation\nL3: \nL4: 【0†Who we are】 【1†What we do】 【2†News】 【3†Talks】 【4†Contact】\nL5: \nL6: 【5†Sponsor】\nL7: # Today's PHP Security Release, and How We're Strengthening PHP Security\nL8: \nL9: Published on Oct 21, 2026\nL10: \nL11: 【6†The PHP Foundation】\nL12: \nL13: 【7†News】 【8†Security】\nL14: Earlier today, the PHP project released PHP 8.5.12, 8.4.27, 8.3.36, and 8.2.35. These releases fix two critical vulnerabilities that, under the right conditions, allow an unauthenticated attacker to execute code on servers running PHP. If you run PHP, it is imperative that you update now. The 【9†announcement on php.net†www.php.net】 contains and references technical details on the vulnerabilities, attack vectors, and instructions on how to update on which systems.\nL15: This post isn't about how to update. It's about how these vulnerabilities were found and fixed, what has changed in how PHP security is handled over the past two years, and what we need from the organizations that depend on PHP to keep PHP's security work going.\nL16: ## Two related critical remote code execution vulnerabilities\nL17:   * CVE-2026-109618 / 【10†GHSA-w8hw-w9fx-j79w†github.com】: RCE via OOM corruption (out-of-memory): If a request reached PHP's memory_limit at exactly the wrong moment, PHP could leave partially initialized internal data behind and later treat it as valid, corrupting memory. On Apache with mod_php, an unauthenticated request can trigger this before any application code runs, and full remote code execution was demonstrated with default settings.\nL18: Remote code execution was also confirmed on PHP-FPM through application-level code paths.\nL19:   * CVE-2026-109619 / 【11†GHSA-6wmf-4rv5-p3rp†github.com】: RCE via stale global state: A fatal error at the wrong point could leave internal state pointing to memory that belonged to a previous request. A later request handled by the same worker could then use that freed memory. A use-after-free and worker crash were confirmed, and the reporter demonstrated remote code execution against a real-world application running on Apache with mod_php.\nL20: Both issues sit in parts of PHP that nearly every request touches: memory management and error handling. Their central place in PHP is what made both vulnerabilities serious, and also so hard to fix without breaking compatibility, hurting performance, or forcing changes on the large ecosystem of third-party extensions.\nL21: ## A first external audit and increased security focus\nL22: In 2024, PHP underwent its first 【12†external security audit】 in years. The PHP Foundation secured funding from the Sovereign Tech Agency to organize this audit through 【13†OSTIF†ostif.org】, and it was carried out by 【14†Quarkslab†www.quarkslab.com】. While fixing one of the issues Quarkslab reported, a PHP core developer contracted by the PHP Foundation found the first signs of the OOM problem behind CVE-2026-109618.\nL23: In 2024, a potential exploit for the OOM problem was theorized, but no concrete vectors were found and other concrete issues with clear impact overshadowed these concerns.\nL24: To fix the OOM problem, the PHP security team developed several competing approaches, but each came with its own trade-offs: backward compatibility breaks, new denial-of-service potential, or requiring changes to many third-party extensions which seemed impossible to coordinate. The PHP security team, operating on consensus, struggled to choose between these and feared the repercussions of shipping the wrong choice.\nL25: Combined with the lack of a known path to remote code execution and the small team of core developers with the necessary deep knowledge of the engine occupied with many responsibilities, this decision process caused work on a fix to stall and restart repeatedly over months. We won't pretend otherwise: the delay was a structural failure, caused by both the security team's decision process and its lack of resources.\nL26: The security team has since started changing its processes, and that work continues, so that decisions can be made by majority and compromises are encouraged.\nL27: ## Building a sustainable professional security organization\nL28: Over the last months the way PHP security work is organized has changed considerably. In May 2026, the PHP Foundation created the 【15†Ecosystem Security Team】, which works alongside the PHP security team on triage, coordination, and hardening. Volker Dusch's work as AI Security Engineer in Residence at the PHP Foundation has significantly improved communication and collaboration between the PHP security team and the Foundation.\nL29: 【16†Alexandre Daubois joined】 the PHP Foundation in July to help the PHP security team triage, handle, and fix security reports and 【17†Daniel Scherzer joined】 in September to take on hardening work.\nL30: Earlier this year, we worked with 【18†Alpha-Omega†alpha-omega.dev】 to scan PHP with Anthropic's Claude Mythos. But since the Mythos scan, we have added other tools, including OpenAI's Daybreak Red for validating reports and exploits, alongside sanitizer builds and reproduction environments that match our CI on every platform PHP supports.\nL31: The PHP Foundation got the PHP project into the 【19†Patch the Planet†openai.com】 program, as part of which security firm 【20†Calif†calif.io】 began an audit of PHP on July 30th, 2026. On August 11th, researcher Ryan A at Calif reported an unconditional remote code execution with the same root cause as the OOM problem we had found in 2024.\nL32: 【21†OpenAI†openai.com】 independently reported the same root cause, followed by further attack vectors from OpenAI on September 18th and from 【22†Wiz†www.wiz.io】 on August 24th. More recently, 【23†Trail of Bits†trailofbits.com】 has also been auditing PHP. The reports from Calif, OpenAI, and Wiz made the full impact of the OOM problem clear, and from August 11th onward, fixing it became the top priority for all of the PHP Foundation's team working on security.\nL33: Without the Foundation’s funding it would have been impossible to have multiple people work on this issue full time, eventually through weekends and around the clock to coordinate and communicate with other involved parties.\nL34: The growth of AI-assisted research also means the PHP security team receives far more reports than before. As Alexandre Daubois described last week in 【24†How the PHP Security Team Handles Your Reports】, the team has published 15 CVEs since July 1st and worked through more than 100 reports that turned out to be invalid, duplicates, or not reproducible. AI helps the team validate reports and group reports sharing the same root cause, but a person makes every call and works on every patch.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPHP2Shell: AI uncovers critical RCE vulnerabilities affecting 70% of the Internet | Wiz Blog (https://www.wiz.io/blog/php2shell-php-rce)\nciteturn89view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.wiz.io/blog/php2shell-php-rce\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 257\nL0: 【0†Wiz】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Blog】\nL3: \nL4: # PHP2Shell: AI uncovers critical RCE vulnerabilities affecting 70% of the Internet\nL5: \nL6: Core PHP engine flaws enable remote code execution and Heartbleed-style memory disclosure across the web.\nL7: \nL8: 【2†PHP2Shell: What you need to know†www.php2shell.com】【3†PHP2Shell: Technical Threat Briefing w/ Wiz Research】\nL9: \nL10: 【4†Sagi Tzadik】, 【5†Shir Tamari】, 【6†Nir Ohfeld】, 【7†Tomer Schwartz】\nL11: \nL12: October 21, 2026|\nL13: \nL14: 【8†Image†www.datocms-assets.com】\nL15: #\nL16: \nL17: Executive Summary\nL18: \nL19:   * Wiz Research uncovered multiple critical vulnerabilities in core PHP, a popular general-purpose scripting language that powers 70% of all websites in the world. Successful exploitation could allow attackers to take control of affected servers, steal sensitive data, and deface the websites running on them.\nL20: \nL21:   * PHP is a backbone of the web: it’s the programming language powering WordPress, Joomla, Drupal, Wikipedia’s MediaWiki, and many others.\nL22:   * These vulnerabilities include memory disclosure and unauthenticated remote code execution in PHP itself, independent of the application running on top of it, putting every affected website in immediate risk.\nL23: \nL24:   * Every PHP version released in the last decade is affected by at least one of these vulnerabilities, going back to version 5.4. Attackers can exploit them remotely, in some cases fairly easily. Immediate patching is strongly advised.\nL25:   * On October 21, 2026, 【9†PHP released the patch†www.php.net】 fixing multiple memory-safety vulnerabilities in the core engine.\nL26:   * These findings rank among the most severe vulnerabilities discovered in PHP, and arguably in software history. The underlying flaws had been present for decades, but demonstrating their exploitability  required an exceptionally complex chain of steps, at the edge of what human researchers could practically achieve. Frontier AI models substantially reduced that barrier, turning a longstanding flaw into an exploit.\nL27: #\nL28: \nL29: Overview\nL30: \nL31: In this post, we explain how frontier AI helped us find and fix vulnerabilities that had remained hidden in the PHP codebase for more than a decade. The issues described below all stem from key architecture decisions, some made over a decade ago, and were grouped under two CVEs, with severity reaching CVSS 9.2. The work grew into a cross-industry effort to protect customers, find variants, deploy detections and mitigations, and verify a patch in time for coordinated disclosure.\nL32: Nearly every PHP version is affected by at least one of these issues, including releases dating back to PHP 5.4. The vulnerabilities can allow unauthenticated attackers to execute code on affected servers or read sensitive process memory. That memory may contain passwords, authentication tokens, API keys and certificates belonging to other requests or the server itself.\nL33: PHP is the programming language behind much of the web, powering WordPress, Drupal and MediaWiki, the software that runs Wikipedia. A flaw in its core engine can affect websites across this ecosystem, even when their own application code is secure.\nL34: Some vulnerabilities become turning points because they challenge fundamental assumptions about security. In 2014, Heartbleed exposed the fragility of shared infrastructure securing the internet. In 2021, Log4Shell revealed how deeply embedded software dependencies could create widespread exposure that organizations struggled to identify. We believe PHP2Shell could mark a similar turning point for the AI era, illustrating how AI can change the practical exploitability of longstanding vulnerabilities.\nL35: Building the exploit required chaining multiple flaws and adapting to each target’s memory layout through repeated testing and debugging. Frontier models were critical to making this process practical, enabling us to develop and adapt working exploits across multiple applications in hours. As the time and expertise required for this work decrease, complexity may no longer provide a meaningful barrier, changing how we define what is vulnerable in practice.\nL36: Wiz Research would like to thank the PHP team, Calif and OpenAI for their work on discovery, coordination and patch verification. 【10†OpenAI†openai.com】 and 【11†Calif†calif.io】 also published blogs containing details on the vulnerability – its history, technical details, and insight into the disclosure process. 【12†PHP†thephp.foundation】 posted as well.\nL37: We’d also like to express our sincere gratitude towards Automattic, Akamai, AWS, 【13†Cloudflare†blog.cloudflare.com】, 【14†Fastly†www.fastly.com】, 【15†Google†cloud.google.com】, Imperva, Microsoft, 【16†Vercel†vercel.com】, and many others, for helping prepare guidance and out-of-the-box mitigations for customers who cannot apply the patch immediately.\nL38: #\nL39: \nL40: The Vulnerabilities\nL41: \nL42: All of the disclosed vulnerabilities are memory-safety issues that stem from bugs in how PHP handles fatal errors while processing requests, particularly out-of-memory errors. These bugs can leave a corrupted internal PHP state behind after a request ends unexpectedly. A later request or worker can reuse that stale state, leading to memory disclosure or corruption.\nL43: Below you can find a table that demonstrates what type of impact was demonstrated by each of the bugs we reported to PHP. The applications listed below are targets we chose for the RCE demonstrations. They are representative targets - exploitation does not require one of these CMSs, as the vulnerable code is in PHP itself.\nL44: Tracking ID  | Bug  | What an attacker can achieve  | What we demonstrated\nL45: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL46: CVE-2026-109618  | Upload-progress unserializer use-after-free Remote process-memory disclosure  | Corrupt process memory and execute code Unauthenticated disclosure of PHP worker memory  | Pre-authentication RCE on WordPress, Drupal and Joomla Application-agnostic remote memory disclosure\nL47: CVE-2026-109619  | Fatal-error backtrace use-after-free OPcache type confusion  | Corrupt process memory and execute code Corrupt shared PHP state and execute code  | Pre-authentication RCE on Drupal Pre-authentication RCE on WordPress\nL48: While this blog covers only four instances of exploitable bugs (grouped under two CVEs) that follow this problematic pattern, there are other tracked, and likely untracked, variants of this issue in the PHP codebase. These variants have different requirements for exploitation and can lead to different types of impact. In any case, the patch provided by the PHP team fixes the issue at its core and eliminates all variants in the patched release.\nL49: #\nL50: \nL51: Impact\nL52: \nL53: We combined these vulnerabilities into a single exploit script, php2shell, and demonstrated that it could execute code remotely without authentication on deployments of WordPress (`wordpress:latest`), Drupal (`drupal:latest`), and Joomla (`joomla:latest`), using their latest container images at the time of testing.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKriittisiä haavoittuvuuksia PHP:ssa | Traficom (https://kyberturvallisuuskeskus.fi/fi/haavoittuvuudet/haavoittuvuus-2026-37)\nciteturn89view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://kyberturvallisuuskeskus.fi/fi/haavoittuvuudet/haavoittuvuus-2026-37\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 246\nL0: 【0†Siirry pääsisältöön】\nL1: \nL2:   * 【1†Traficom.fi Ulkoinen verkkopalvelu.†traficom.fi】\nL3: \nL4: 【2†Kyberturvallisuuskeskus】\nL5: \nL6: Hae\nL7: \nL8: Sulje\nL9: \nL10: Hae\nL11: \nL12: Hae\nL13: \nL14: [Input: Mitä etsit?]\nL15: \nL16: Hae\nL17: \nL18: Hae\nL19: \nL20: Suomeksi\nL21: \nL22: Sulje\nL23: \nL24: Suomeksi\nL25: \nL26: Suomeksi\nL27: \nL28:   * Suomeksi\nL29: \nL30: Suomeksi\nL31: \nL32:   * På svenska\nL33: \nL34: Denna sida finns inte på svenska.\nL35: \nL36:   * In English\nL37: \nL38: This page is not available in English.\nL39: \nL40: Valikko\nL41: \nL42: Sulje\nL43: \nL44: 【3†Ilmoita tietoturvaloukkauksesta】\nL45: \nL46: 【3†Ilmoita tietoturvaloukkauksesta】\nL47: \nL48:   * På svenska\nL49: \nL50: Denna sida finns inte